WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.920
Your genome holds about 518 genes for protein kinases.

2
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.320
Nearly every one of these enzymes runs the same reaction.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.180
It takes a phosphate from ATP and fastens it to a protein.

4
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.060
Why would cells lean so heavily on one small chemical group?

5
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.800
What makes a phosphate a good switch?

6
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:33.713
ATP sits at millimolar concentration in the cell, so a kinase never waits for its

7
00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:39.893
donor. The kinase takes the end phosphate off ATP, leaving ADP.

8
00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:46.093
In animals, it fastens that phosphate to a side chain that ends in a hydroxyl group.

9
00:00:46.673 --> 00:00:49.913
That means serine, threonine or tyrosine.

10
00:00:50.693 --> 00:00:56.793
Near pH 7, the phosphate carries close to two negative charges, on a spot that had

11
00:00:56.793 --> 00:00:57.793
none.

12
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:03.633
That charge matters, because a folded protein is held in shape by charges that

13
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:05.533
attract and repel each other.

14
00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:09.113
Few changes this small disturb it this much.

15
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:15.073
Every copy of a protein flickers among several arrangements, some working and some

16
00:01:15.073 --> 00:01:21.153
not. A phosphate makes the arrangements that suit its charge cheaper to occupy, so

17
00:01:21.153 --> 00:01:22.693
more copies sit in them.

18
00:01:23.313 --> 00:01:28.473
In glycogen phosphorylase, that shifts the enzyme toward its active form, yet the

19
00:01:28.473 --> 00:01:30.853
same trick inhibits glycogen synthase.

20
00:01:31.616 --> 00:01:37.276
A phosphatase takes the phosphate off again, with water, and leaves the protein

21
00:01:37.276 --> 00:01:38.796
exactly as it was.

22
00:01:39.456 --> 00:01:42.136
It does not run the kinase reaction backwards.

23
00:01:42.776 --> 00:01:46.476
It runs a different reaction, and each one goes downhill.

24
00:01:47.616 --> 00:01:51.556
Think of a whiteboard, where the marker and the eraser are different tools.

25
00:01:52.436 --> 00:01:58.596
The cell likewise regulates adding and removing independently, at the price of one ATP

26
00:01:58.596 --> 00:01:59.716
per round trip.

27
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.690
Why does a cell need phosphatases at all?

28
00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:09.130
Think of a sink, with the tap running and the drain open, where the water level

29
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:15.010
depends on both. In the same way, the fraction of protein carrying a phosphate

30
00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.890
depends on both enzymes.

31
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:22.890
A signal can raise that level by activating the kinase, or by inhibiting the

32
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.070
phosphatase. Cells do both.

33
00:02:26.483 --> 00:02:29.803
A phosphate makes a good switch for four reasons.

34
00:02:30.463 --> 00:02:34.303
It adds charge, and it stays until an enzyme removes it.

35
00:02:35.083 --> 00:02:40.023
Different enzymes add it and remove it, and ATP keeps the kinase supplied.

36
00:02:40.903 --> 00:02:42.003
So keep this rule.

37
00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:48.303
A kinase writes the phosphate, a phosphatase erases it, and the balance of the two

38
00:02:48.303 --> 00:02:49.203
sets the level.
